From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anonymous <anonymous@localhost>
Date: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 01:20:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] torrent_info: load SHA-1 piece hashes on demand

torrent_info::load_piece_hashes_on_demand(loader, keep_loaded) moves the v1
piece hashes, usually ~99% of a torrent's metadata, out of memory. The info
section kept in memory gets an empty "pieces" string (file names and other
borrowed pointers are rebased onto it). hash_for_piece(), hash_for_piece_ptr()
and info_section() load the full section through the client's loader, check it
against the info-hash, and keep it, shared by all copies of the object, until
release_unused_piece_hashes(idle) finds it idle. A failed load throws
system_error (no_metadata), which puts the torrent into an error state instead
of failing its pieces. info_section_size() gives the size without loading.

To avoid thrashing, a torrent whose hashes are needed again within the idle
time they were released after waits twice as long before the next release (up
to 16 times); a reload after a longer pause halves it again.

ut_metadata only needs the size for its handshake and request checks, so it
no longer loads the section for them, and its on_piece_pass() no longer reads
it for nothing (a leftover from when it copied the metadata).

A seed that is not in seed mode only needs its hashes for a recheck or to write
out its metadata, so a client can keep them on disk for idle torrents.
